# Lemma ingress.
#
# Three hostnames, and the split between them is a security boundary rather
# than a matter of taste:
#
#   https://<domain>              the UI
#   https://<domain>:<api port>   the API
#   https://<slug>.apps.<domain>  pod apps — user-authored HTML
#
# The UI and the API share one hostname on two ports on purpose. Ports do not
# participate in cookie host matching, so a *host-only* session cookie (see
# SESSION_COOKIE_DOMAIN= in .env) is sent to both, and is never sent to an app
# subdomain. Putting the API on api.<domain> instead would force
# SESSION_COOKIE_DOMAIN=.<domain>, which hands every pod app the operator's
# session. lemma-stack reaches the same conclusion for desktop, at length, in
# lemma_stack/config/render.py.
#
# Nothing here rewrites paths or invents headers. AppHostRoutingMiddleware
# resolves an app from the Host header itself and has a branch specifically for
# a proxy that does not do the rewriting (app/modules/apps/api/host_routing.py).
# The cloud nginx ingress does rewrite, and both are supported; this one simply
# passes Host through, which is the smaller contract to keep working.
#
# LEMMA_TLS_ARG is written by bootstrap.sh: `internal` (Caddy's own CA — the
# default, and the only thing that can work without a real domain) or a Let's
# Encrypt account email. LEMMA_APPS_TLS_OPTS is `on_demand` in the second case
# and empty in the first, because Caddy's internal CA can sign a wildcard
# outright while a public one cannot without DNS-01.
{
	# Only consulted when the apps site uses on-demand TLS, which is only when
	# this deployment has a real domain. Answered by the endpoint at the bottom.
	on_demand_tls {
		ask http://localhost:9007/check
	}
}

# Streaming is the reason this snippet exists. Agent output, run events and
# every SSE endpoint have to reach the browser as they are produced, so
# responses are flushed rather than buffered, and a read timeout measured in
# seconds would cut a long agent run off mid-answer.
(lemma_upstream) {
	flush_interval -1
	transport http {
		read_timeout 1h
		write_timeout 1h
		dial_timeout 5s
	}
}

# ── UI ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
https://{$LEMMA_DOMAIN} {
	tls {$LEMMA_TLS_ARG}
	request_body {
		max_size {$LEMMA_MAX_UPLOAD:512MB}
	}

	reverse_proxy frontend:8080 {
		import lemma_upstream
	}
}

# ── API ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
https://{$LEMMA_DOMAIN}:{$LEMMA_API_PORT} {
	tls {$LEMMA_TLS_ARG}
	request_body {
		max_size {$LEMMA_MAX_UPLOAD:512MB}
	}

	reverse_proxy api:8000 {
		import lemma_upstream
	}
}

# ── Pod apps ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# One certificate per app hostname, issued the first time somebody loads it.
# A wildcard certificate would need a DNS-01 challenge, which needs a DNS
# provider plugin compiled into Caddy; on-demand issuance needs neither.
https://*.apps.{$LEMMA_DOMAIN} {
	tls {$LEMMA_TLS_ARG} {
		{$LEMMA_APPS_TLS_OPTS}
	}
	request_body {
		max_size {$LEMMA_MAX_UPLOAD:512MB}
	}

	reverse_proxy api:8000 {
		import lemma_upstream
	}
}

# ── on-demand TLS gate ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Caddy refuses on-demand issuance without one of these, and it is right to:
# without it, anyone who points a DNS record at this machine can make it ask a
# certificate authority for a certificate on demand. This one answers 200 only
# for hostnames under the apps domain.
#
# The port is not published in docker-compose.yml, so it is reachable only from
# the container network Caddy shares with the api and the frontend — and it
# only answers to the Host `localhost`, which is what Caddy asks itself.
http://localhost:9007 {
	@app_host expression {query.domain}.endsWith({env.LEMMA_APPS_SUFFIX})
	respond @app_host 200
	respond 403
}
